cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use EncoTESS, please cite both the software and the accompanying
  paper: Van-Lane et al. (2026), "EncoTESS: Age-Sensitive Encodings from Raw
  TESS Light Curves", arXiv:2608.25019, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2608.25019.
type: software
title: "EncoTESS: latent light-curve encoder for TESS 2-min photometry"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-28"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23738984"
authors:
  - family-names: Van-Lane
    given-names: Phil
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-4567-9946"
    affiliation: "University of Toronto; University of California San Diego"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/philvanlane/encotess"
abstract: >-
  EncoTESS turns a 2-minute-cadence TESS light curve into a 1536-dimensional
  latent encoding. From that summary you can compare stars, explore structure
  across the sample, forecast the light curve a few steps ahead, or perform
  downstream tasks such as age inference. This release includes the trained
  encoder, the fitted PCA and PLS projections, a demonstration age-inference
  model, and the encodings and metadata for every light curve in the sample.
keywords:
  - astronomy
  - TESS
  - light curves
  - autoencoder
  - normalizing flows
